    description: "FOUND 2026-10-28 by WI-foloh's premise measurement (production CLI, dev 3c53837bb5, fixture in the session scratchpad).\n\npy.py does not type the readers asyncio's stream factories return, so their reads reach NO catalogue row at all:\n  reader, writer = await asyncio.open_unix_connection(path); await reader.read(100)   -> python:external:0-0:read\n  reader, writer = await asyncio.open_connection(host, 80); await reader.readuntil(b\"\\n\") -> python:external:0-0:readuntil\n  proc = await asyncio.create_subprocess_exec(..., stdout=PIPE); await proc.stdout.read() -> python:external:0-0:read\nOnly an annotated parameter (reader: asyncio.StreamReader) reaches the asyncio.StreamReader rows (net_recv). So a TCP read through open_connection is not a net_recv crossing at all today: a recall gap, larger than the medium question WI-foloh asks.\n\nWHAT IS MISSING (two mechanisms py.py does not have):\n 1. Typing a TUPLE-unpacked, AWAITED producer: library_signatures values are one type, and the assignment typing (_external_constructor_type / _bound_call_type) reads a Call value, not Await(Call), and a Name target, not a Tuple. open_connection / open_unix_connection return (StreamReader, StreamWriter); create_subprocess_exec returns a Process whose .stdout is a StreamReader.\n 2. An io_target_kind stamp: py.py stamps none anywhere (the typing.TextIO dual rows select only their first row in practice). The dual row WI-foloh wants (net_recv + ipc_recv, call_site_undecidable, abstains_to net_recv) needs a pipe stamp from open_unix_connection / create_subprocess_* and a net_stream one from open_connection.\n\nUntil 1 lands, an ipc_recv twin has no producer (LIVE rule 7), so WI-foloh waits on this."  # 1390
